1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,620
For thousands of years, they've provided us with everything from gum, to stamps, to holy

2
00:00:13,621 --> 00:00:20,460
water, offering better entertainment, communication, and transportation.

3
00:00:20,461 --> 00:00:26,880
These coin-operated machines can be addictive, annoying, and sometimes really weird.

4
00:00:26,881 --> 00:00:29,840
This is made by a San Quentin prisoner.

5
00:00:29,841 --> 00:00:33,080
said to contribute to an immoral society.

6
00:00:33,081 --> 00:00:36,840
And today, they contribute to billion-dollar industries.

7
00:00:36,841 --> 00:00:40,520
You'll never think of that loose change the same way again

8
00:00:40,521 --> 00:00:41,620
on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,300
Small change is big business.

10
00:00:58,301 --> 00:01:02,000
And vending machines are the biggest source of revenue in the coin-operated industry.

11
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:05,640
A hundred million people use them every day.

12
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:09,680
Most stay open 24 hours, seven days a week.

13
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:15,340
And you can buy anything, from aspirin to ice cream.

14
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:22,300
But the most common vending machines are those that dispense drinks and snacks.

15
00:01:22,301 --> 00:01:30,500
The food and refreshment vending industry in the United States is approximately $37 billion a year,

16
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:36,420
and 100 million Americans a day walk up to nearly 5 million machines and make a purchase.

17
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:43,280
There are approximately a million people employed in the industry and over 13,500 companies providing service.

18
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:50,280
Making the job of those who maintain vending machines more efficient is having technology at the tip of their fingers.

19
00:01:50,281 --> 00:01:53,960
Here at Ace Vending, we use handheld computers.

20
00:01:53,961 --> 00:01:56,660
What the driver does is open the machine,

21
00:01:56,661 --> 00:01:59,780
plugs into the computer board of the vending machine,

22
00:01:59,781 --> 00:02:02,520
downloads the financial and sales data,

23
00:02:02,521 --> 00:02:06,360
and then we collect that data, bring it back to the warehouse.

24
00:02:06,361 --> 00:02:10,100
Every time the drivers finish their rounds,

25
00:02:10,101 --> 00:02:13,240
they download the data into the company's computer system.

26
00:02:13,241 --> 00:02:17,800
This gives an exact accounting of the money collected

27
00:02:17,801 --> 00:02:19,440
and of every product sold.

28
00:02:19,441 --> 00:02:24,440
By continually updating the sales history of each machine,

29
00:02:24,441 --> 00:02:27,440
warehouse workers are able to accurately fill containers

30
00:02:27,441 --> 00:02:29,440
with the anticipated restocking needs of the machines

31
00:02:29,441 --> 00:02:31,440
on each driver's route.

32
00:02:31,441 --> 00:02:36,440
And what that does is prevents him from coming in twice

33
00:02:36,441 --> 00:02:39,440
by coming in and looking in and seeing what the vending machine

34
00:02:39,441 --> 00:02:41,440
needs and going back to the truck and getting it.

35
00:02:41,441 --> 00:02:44,440
It's already preloaded for him based on the download.

36
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:48,440
Vending machine technology has come a long way.

37
00:02:48,441 --> 00:02:56,140
Scholars believe that the first vending machine was a holy water dispenser, used in Greek

38
00:02:56,141 --> 00:03:00,580
temples dating back to 215 BCE.

39
00:03:00,581 --> 00:03:05,360
When deposited in the machine, the weight of the coin caused a lever to lower and dispense

40
00:03:05,361 --> 00:03:08,740
a small amount of the water.

41
00:03:08,741 --> 00:03:17,340
Today's vending machines can be complex, able to take all kinds of payments, deliver change,

42
00:03:17,341 --> 00:03:19,840
offer an almost dizzying variety of goodies.

43
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:26,280
At Crane Merchandising's enormous 500,000 square foot

44
00:03:26,281 --> 00:03:30,580
facility in St. Louis, snack and drink machines are born.

45
00:03:30,581 --> 00:03:34,660
The process starts with large rolls of steel sheeting.

46
00:03:34,661 --> 00:03:40,020
Each coil will manufacture about 125 to 150 machines.

47
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:43,460
We will mount the steel, the coil steel onto the dual

48
00:03:43,461 --> 00:03:48,689
mandrel and the mandrel will take it over to a decoiling process.

49
00:03:48,690 --> 00:03:50,700
There's a deep impression

50
00:03:50,701 --> 00:03:56,580
into the floor and we'll take the bend out and then we shear it to about 15 feet in length.

51
00:03:56,581 --> 00:04:00,870
They move from there to a punching, notching station.

52
00:04:00,871 --> 00:04:03,460
From there they move into a roll

53
00:04:03,461 --> 00:04:08,220
forming station and the front and the back edge of the cabinet are rolled on and once

54
00:04:08,221 --> 00:04:11,880
they've left there they move into the welding station where the components for the inside

55
00:04:11,881 --> 00:04:14,480
to the cabinet or added.

56
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:17,440
The next stop is the tangent vending station,

57
00:04:17,441 --> 00:04:20,440
where the long steel sheet is bent to form the top

58
00:04:20,441 --> 00:04:21,840
and two sides of the cabinet.

59
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:27,780
Further down the assembly line, the bottom and the legs

60
00:04:27,781 --> 00:04:30,260
are attached.

61
00:04:30,261 --> 00:04:32,640
Then workers hang the cabinets in various parts

62
00:04:32,641 --> 00:04:34,540
on a moving conveyor that carries them

63
00:04:34,541 --> 00:04:38,140
through a washer and dryer.

64
00:04:38,141 --> 00:04:40,940
Next, it's onto a room where they are coated

65
00:04:40,941 --> 00:04:42,860
with the dry powdered paint.

66
00:04:42,861 --> 00:04:45,580
This line has an electrostatic charge on it

67
00:04:45,581 --> 00:04:48,060
so that when the powder is sprayed on them,

68
00:04:48,061 --> 00:04:50,140
it is like a magnet and holds it on there

69
00:04:50,141 --> 00:04:51,720
while they're being painted.

70
00:04:51,721 --> 00:04:54,460
That charge is held on them until they go into an oven.

71
00:04:54,461 --> 00:04:57,120
They're heated to 450 degrees.

72
00:04:57,121 --> 00:05:00,100
That melts that paint powder, becomes a liquid,

73
00:05:00,101 --> 00:05:02,480
and then no longer needs the electrostatic charge.

74
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:07,080
Once the basic structure of each type of machine

75
00:05:07,081 --> 00:05:10,080
is completed, workers install all the internal

76
00:05:10,081 --> 00:05:14,420
and electronic parts, including the defining electronic coin

77
00:05:14,421 --> 00:05:16,140
mechanisms.

78
00:05:16,141 --> 00:05:17,880
This is the standard coin mechanism.

79
00:05:17,881 --> 00:05:19,560
It sits inside a vending machine.

80
00:05:19,561 --> 00:05:21,540
And when coins are put in through the front of the machine,

81
00:05:21,541 --> 00:05:23,160
it roots them through here.

82
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:25,400
And there's a series of sensors inside.

83
00:05:25,401 --> 00:05:28,000
And it uses this to measure how the coin going through changes

84
00:05:28,001 --> 00:05:28,701
the frequency.

85
00:05:28,702 --> 00:05:31,200
From that, it can very accurately determine thickness,

86
00:05:31,201 --> 00:05:34,140
what the diameter is, what alloy it's made out of.

87
00:05:34,141 --> 00:05:37,780
Used to be lots of problems with frauds and counterfeit coins

88
00:05:37,781 --> 00:05:38,700
going through.

89
00:05:38,701 --> 00:05:42,360
That more or less disappeared with the advent of this electronic recognition technology.

90
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:46,920
And to avoid losing sales from consumers not carrying enough change,

91
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:49,540
most of today's vending machines have options.

92
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:52,100
Paper money or a credit card.

93
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:55,540
Virtually, every vending machine has a bill validator.

94
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:59,260
It recognizes bills by shining light through it,

95
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,300
little LEDs that go through it, known points on here.

96
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:06,760
And depending on the ink and the paper that had been made by the mint,

97
00:06:06,761 --> 00:06:08,160
It'll know that it's a good bill.

98
00:06:08,161 --> 00:06:09,700
It'll know what denomination is.

99
00:06:09,701 --> 00:06:11,360
It also checks magnetic inks.

100
00:06:11,361 --> 00:06:12,960
Many people don't realize this,

101
00:06:12,961 --> 00:06:16,400
but the black ink on the front of the dollar bill is magnetic.

102
00:06:16,401 --> 00:06:18,400
When manufacturing snack machines,

103
00:06:18,401 --> 00:06:21,640
customer specifications determine the types of metal spirals

104
00:06:21,641 --> 00:06:25,040
to be installed in the shelves.

105
00:06:25,041 --> 00:06:27,480
This is the heart of a snack machine.

106
00:06:27,481 --> 00:06:29,280
You notice they come in different varieties.

107
00:06:29,281 --> 00:06:31,580
This is a 30-count. This is 11-count.

108
00:06:31,581 --> 00:06:34,780
Your 11-count usually produces chips or pastry,

109
00:06:34,781 --> 00:06:38,480
And this one do candy bars and peanuts and things like that.

110
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:43,760
The back end of each spiral is attached to a small 24-volt motor

111
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:47,060
that rotates once to push the selected product forward,

112
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:52,660
dropping it into the tray at the bottom of the machine.

113
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:56,860
Crane Merchandising has come up with an entertaining way

114
00:06:56,861 --> 00:07:00,040
to dispense 20-ounce bottled soft drinks without dropping them.

115
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:04,020
The XY delivery mechanism rises to the height of the shelf,

116
00:07:04,021 --> 00:07:07,560
and delivers the product conveniently to the delivery port.

117
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:10,280
There's a good bit of theater, but most importantly,

118
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:13,460
when the product is delivered, it hasn't agitated the drink,

119
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:15,560
so when I open the cap, it doesn't explode.

120
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:19,460
Some vending machines can dispense more than just soda, candy, and chips.

121
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:22,120
Others can sell you an entire meal.

122
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:28,160
New York City's East Village was home to one of the last giant vending machines in the United States.

123
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:31,180
BAM Automat launched in 2006

124
00:07:31,181 --> 00:07:34,780
and was the talk of the city before the owner moved on to other ventures.

125
00:07:34,781 --> 00:07:36,660
Some people look at it as a vending machine,

126
00:07:36,661 --> 00:07:40,380
but we look at it more like little peep windows for foodies.

127
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:46,140
Unseen by the public, the shelves are being restocked by the kitchen

128
00:07:46,141 --> 00:07:48,460
on the other side of the wall of doors.

129
00:07:48,461 --> 00:07:51,260
The way it works is you get a bunch of coins,

130
00:07:51,261 --> 00:07:53,260
and then you just drop them into the slot.

131
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:59,020
And then you just pull any of these doors with the food in it,

132
00:07:59,021 --> 00:08:00,300
and then you just pull the food out.

133
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:01,880
Voila.

134
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:04,920
The concept of automats isn't new.

135
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:07,820
They were first seen in both New York and Philadelphia.

136
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:11,160
At their peak of popularity in the 1950s,

137
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:13,880
there were 180 automats in the two cities,

138
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:17,120
and they fed more than 800,000 people per day.

139
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:19,920
Back then, they were large restaurants,

140
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:22,600
with dining areas offering a full selection of items

141
00:08:22,601 --> 00:08:24,400
to put together substantial meals.

142
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:28,340
The last of the original automats closed in 1991,

143
00:08:28,341 --> 00:08:31,580
with the explosion of fast food restaurants.

144
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:36,780
Back then, a typical grilled cheese would be probably a nickel.

145
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,260
Now it's $2.

146
00:08:38,860 --> 00:08:41,700
But $2 in today's value, it's worth it.

147
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,500
But while fresh foods and vending machines

148
00:08:44,501 --> 00:08:46,460
were all the rage in the 1950s,

149
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:50,000
over a century ago, all it took was a stick of gum

150
00:08:50,001 --> 00:08:52,540
to launch the U.S. coin-operated vending business.

151
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:58,020
America is home to the most vending machines in the world.

152
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:02,220
with the current estimate of over 7 million coin-operated machines,

153
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:08,800
serving everything from electronics to makeup to chips and soda.

154
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:12,200
According to the National Automated Merchandising Association,

155
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:17,920
consumers worldwide purchased over 88 billion bottled beverages from vending machines last year.

156
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:21,540
But what launched the vending industry in America wasn't a beverage.

157
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:23,820
It was the humble ball of chewing gum.

158
00:09:23,821 --> 00:09:29,820
Today, 20 million Americans chew 16 or more pieces of chewing gum per day.

159
00:09:29,821 --> 00:09:35,820
The Spearmint Stick from Wrigley's Doublemint remains America's leading chewing gum brand.

160
00:09:35,821 --> 00:09:41,820
The OK Manufacturing Company in Salt Lake City proves you can still build a thriving business on gum.

161
00:09:41,821 --> 00:09:47,820
Despite offering a cheaper product, this machine can earn more than any vending machine offering.

162
00:09:47,821 --> 00:09:52,820
Brothers Jeff and Kurt Osler know this well. They entered the business in a big way.

163
00:09:52,821 --> 00:09:55,860
The desired in vending is to capture the quarter.

164
00:09:55,861 --> 00:09:57,200
And in order to capture the quarter,

165
00:09:57,201 --> 00:09:59,260
you have to capture their attention first.

166
00:09:59,261 --> 00:10:01,860
There is nothing better than the big and giant gumball

167
00:10:01,861 --> 00:10:03,360
machine to capture attention.

168
00:10:03,361 --> 00:10:06,500
This machine holds 40,000 pieces of gum.

169
00:10:06,501 --> 00:10:09,260
It stands about 6 foot 10 up here,

170
00:10:09,261 --> 00:10:12,780
holds the 40,000 gumballs, which if we really create in dollars,

171
00:10:12,781 --> 00:10:16,340
that's $10,000 that this machine would dispense out

172
00:10:16,341 --> 00:10:17,580
and put in its cash box.

173
00:10:17,581 --> 00:10:19,420
Our very first time we went and put one out,

174
00:10:19,421 --> 00:10:22,780
three days after we had it there, had over $500 in it.

175
00:10:22,781 --> 00:10:25,540
And that's when we knew we had a great concept.

176
00:10:25,541 --> 00:10:28,780
I used to put my child into the side.

177
00:10:28,781 --> 00:10:30,180
They would go in to collect the machine,

178
00:10:30,181 --> 00:10:34,060
