00:13:02,861 --> 00:13:05,640
coin-operated industry.

221
00:13:05,641 --> 00:13:09,680
Arcade games and amusements.

222
00:13:12,140 --> 00:13:13,700
For more than a century,

223
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:16,900
it's been a hypnotic realm of flashing lights,

224
00:13:18,140 --> 00:13:19,020
crazy mechanics,

225
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:21,260
and freaky visuals.

226
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:26,380
Before radio and TV,

227
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:29,800
Penny Arcades offered the public a variety of entertainment,

228
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:32,420
including the earliest forms of moving pictures.

229
00:13:32,421 --> 00:13:38,920
This is an early motion picture machine built in the late 1800s.

230
00:13:38,921 --> 00:13:41,360
It's called a clamshell mutoscope.

231
00:13:41,361 --> 00:13:44,000
It's what they used before film.

232
00:13:44,001 --> 00:13:48,680
And if I open this up, I can show you the insides.

233
00:13:48,681 --> 00:13:56,140
And it's actually the coin that starts the mechanism, which activates the flip cards.

234
00:13:56,141 --> 00:13:58,640
The person operating the machine is the motor.

235
00:13:58,641 --> 00:14:02,960
The faster you turn the handle, the faster the picture will go by.

236
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:13,840
The flip cards do one complete rotation, 360 degrees, which is approximately 1,500 cards, and then it shuts off.

237
00:14:16,280 --> 00:14:21,100
Mutoscopes were soon replaced with kinetoscopes, invented by Thomas Edison and William Dixon.

238
00:14:21,101 --> 00:14:25,100
The first kinetoscope parlor opened in 1894,

239
00:14:25,101 --> 00:14:27,100
offering the public its first chance

240
00:14:27,101 --> 00:14:30,100
to see motion pictures on film.

241
00:14:30,101 --> 00:14:32,100
One side of the cabinet contained a battery,

242
00:14:32,101 --> 00:14:34,100
that powered a small motor,

243
00:14:34,101 --> 00:14:36,100
and on the other side, beyond a wooden panel,

244
00:14:36,101 --> 00:14:39,100
a projection lamp that 50 feet of film

245
00:14:39,101 --> 00:14:40,100
that ran through the machine.

246
00:14:40,101 --> 00:14:42,100
But when Edison perfected the movie projector,

247
00:14:42,101 --> 00:14:45,100
he made kinetoscopes instantly obsolete.

248
00:14:45,101 --> 00:14:45,801
obsolete.

249
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,640
From arm wrestling a mechanical brute,

250
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:56,060
to trying to keep up with a virtual jump rope,

251
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:59,420
from those creepy old fortune tellers,

252
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:01,420
to dancing cowboys,

253
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:04,600
coin-operated machines have been amusing

254
00:15:04,601 --> 00:15:06,760
and entertaining us for more than a century.

255
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:11,400
Coin-operated music machines first appeared

256
00:15:11,401 --> 00:15:12,740
in the late 1800s.

257
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:14,760
Some of the earliest were basically

258
00:15:14,761 --> 00:15:15,760
big music boxes.

259
00:15:15,761 --> 00:15:24,200
But soon, many became very elaborate technological fantasies.

260
00:15:24,201 --> 00:15:29,880
The mechanics were often a big part of the entertainment.

261
00:15:29,881 --> 00:15:32,880
This is a Seberg Nickelodeon from the early 1900s.

262
00:15:32,881 --> 00:15:37,300
I'll open it up so you can see what's inside.

263
00:15:37,301 --> 00:15:42,260
When you put your quarter in, it drops into a slot which operates an electrical switch

264
00:15:42,261 --> 00:15:44,600
which sends a signal to the electric motor,

265
00:15:44,601 --> 00:15:48,560
and all of its function came from the paper roll.

266
00:15:48,561 --> 00:15:52,520
In this particular instrument, we have piano,

267
00:15:52,521 --> 00:15:55,200
and we have a set of violin pipes,

268
00:15:55,201 --> 00:15:57,360
and we also have what's called a mandolin rail,

269
00:15:57,361 --> 00:16:00,120
which makes the piano sound like a honky-tonk piano.

270
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:03,220
When the tune is completed,

271
00:16:03,221 --> 00:16:05,480
this perforation crosses the tracker bar,

272
00:16:05,481 --> 00:16:08,500
sends a pneumatic signal to the electrical switch,

273
00:16:08,501 --> 00:16:10,420
and drops the coin into the coin box.

274
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:21,180
Pay phonographs became popular in the early 1900s and were the precursors to the jukebox,

275
00:16:21,181 --> 00:16:24,360
which first appeared in the 1920s.

276
00:16:24,361 --> 00:16:30,880
Companies like Rockola are still making jukeboxes, but with a 21st century flair.

277
00:16:30,881 --> 00:16:35,700
The first jukeboxes played the very large 78 RPM records, just one side.

278
00:16:35,701 --> 00:16:41,140
And of course that transcended into the 45 RPM record with a big hole in it in about

279
00:16:41,141 --> 00:16:49,860
1949 and then eventually the CDs came in the mid 80s but this is transcended from CDs over to a

280
00:16:49,861 --> 00:16:56,180
jukebox that is connected to the internet literally plays hundreds of thousands of CDs this is a

281
00:16:56,181 --> 00:17:02,980
touchscreen you can just go on it touch what you would like and it blasted out of this jukebox with

282
00:17:02,981 --> 00:17:09,700
900 watts of power jukeboxes have managed to keep up with the times but more coins are inserted into

283
00:17:09,701 --> 00:17:13,480
to another part of the amusement business, gaming.

284
00:17:13,481 --> 00:17:17,800
In fact, at its peak, customers spend over $8 billion a year,

285
00:17:17,801 --> 00:17:20,100
mainly due to the lure of video games.

286
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:23,460
This is an example of one of the first successful

287
00:17:23,461 --> 00:17:25,760
coin-operated Pong-style video games.

288
00:17:25,761 --> 00:17:27,840
It's very primitive by today's standards,

289
00:17:27,841 --> 00:17:31,820
but by 1972 standards, this was ahead of its time.

290
00:17:31,821 --> 00:17:33,800
Everyone was very impressed to be able to play a game

291
00:17:33,801 --> 00:17:35,760
on a TV set instead of just watching it.

292
00:17:35,761 --> 00:17:38,500
And then the microprocessor made the game

293
00:17:38,501 --> 00:17:40,460
10 times more complex and amazing.

294
00:17:40,461 --> 00:17:43,700
And with the advent of games like Pac-Man a few years later,

295
00:17:43,701 --> 00:17:46,000
the public got really excited.

296
00:17:46,001 --> 00:17:48,000
Some players were even more excited

297
00:17:48,001 --> 00:17:50,080
when they found out they could pool the simple toy

298
00:17:50,081 --> 00:17:52,320
mechanisms used back then.

299
00:17:52,321 --> 00:17:55,900
In the late 70s and early 80s, when video games came out,

300
00:17:55,901 --> 00:17:57,540
people became so creative.

301
00:17:57,541 --> 00:18:00,400
They would go and drill holes through their quarters,

302
00:18:00,401 --> 00:18:02,600
tie a little bit of fishing line in there,

303
00:18:02,601 --> 00:18:05,440
put it in till it hit the micro switch, pull it back out,

304
00:18:05,441 --> 00:18:07,440
get their quarter, get a free game.

305
00:18:07,441 --> 00:18:10,300
Eventually, the industry wised up and they put cutters in there,

306
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:12,900
where as soon as you tried to pull anything back out,

307
00:18:13,140 --> 00:18:15,700
it would cut that line, quarter would go into the box,

308
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,940
you'd still get your game, but the operator would still get his money.

309
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:24,200
Before video games, pinball was king.

310
00:18:25,580 --> 00:18:31,480
In 1932, there were around 150 pinball machine manufacturers in the U.S.,

311
00:18:31,481 --> 00:18:35,580
but now only one survives, Stern Pinball.

312
00:18:35,581 --> 00:18:38,580
Located in Melrose Park, Illinois,

313
00:18:38,581 --> 00:18:41,580
the company's president, Gary Stern,

314
00:18:41,581 --> 00:18:44,580
has been around pinball most of his life.

315
00:18:44,581 --> 00:18:46,580
And the games his company now produces

316
00:18:46,581 --> 00:18:49,580
are much different than the ones his father used to sell.

317
00:18:51,580 --> 00:18:55,580
This is a pinball machine from the late 60s.

318
00:18:55,581 --> 00:18:58,580
These days they were much simpler games than we make today

319
00:18:58,581 --> 00:19:00,580
with electromechanical logic,

320
00:19:00,581 --> 00:19:04,580
relay step-up units, much like in an old telephone system.

321
00:19:04,581 --> 00:19:06,580
You can't make something this simple today.

322
00:19:06,581 --> 00:19:09,820
People are looking for more entertainment, more to do,

323
00:19:09,821 --> 00:19:12,720
much like you can't make cars with roll-up windows.

324
00:19:12,721 --> 00:19:14,420
People want power windows today.

325
00:19:14,421 --> 00:19:17,900
So now we make games with a lot of toys to play.

326
00:19:19,420 --> 00:19:21,760
They're much more complex.

327
00:19:21,761 --> 00:19:24,960
They do a lot more and a lot more entertainment value.

328
00:19:26,660 --> 00:19:29,780
Stern Pinball produces three or four titles a year,

329
00:19:31,440 --> 00:19:32,940
like this Indiana Jones game.

330
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:38,520
Stern's game designer first rendered it into 2D computer graphics.

331
00:19:39,260 --> 00:19:43,380
Then 3D computer artists created virtual versions of the working parts.

332
00:19:44,880 --> 00:19:50,380
Next, Stern built a prototype play field to test the electronics and basic gameplay.

333
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:53,540
Once approved, the game went into production.

334
00:19:54,020 --> 00:19:55,240
This is the cable department.

335
00:19:55,241 --> 00:19:59,900
We use an average of over a half a mile of wire in every pinball machine we manufacture.

336
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:03,020
We have operators that are on the line over here.

337
00:20:03,021 --> 00:20:09,780
It's a progressive line, and each operator will add one wire at a time until a complete wire harness is built.

338
00:20:10,860 --> 00:20:17,220
The wire harnesses travel down assembly lines as workers carefully craft an incredibly complex maze

339
00:20:17,221 --> 00:20:20,080
that includes all the electronic components and lights.

340
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:26,400
On the top of the surface of the playfield, workers attach all of the hardware,

341
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:30,780
including the flippers, bumpers, and the custom-designed props for each game.

342
00:20:30,781 --> 00:20:37,780
By the time they're done, each of Stern's games contain an average of 3,500 parts.

343
00:20:37,781 --> 00:20:39,705
We're in the play field test area.

344
00:20:39,706 --> 00:20:46,780
In this section over here, we test to make sure electrically and mechanically all the components on a play field are working.

345
00:20:46,781 --> 00:20:52,780
You have your flippers down here, you have your ball kickers, shoot the ball back out onto a play field.

346
00:20:52,781 --> 00:20:58,780
The other thing, you have easy access to all the components so you can troubleshoot quicker, you can flip the play field around.

347
00:20:58,781 --> 00:21:02,100
You have complete access to all your electrical and mechanical components,

348
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:03,800
and that's how we troubleshoot them.

349
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:06,260
If everything is working properly,

350
00:21:07,560 --> 00:21:10,160
workers install the completed play fields in the cabinets,

351
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:13,240
fully test the finished games,

352
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,880
and then prepare them for shipment.

353
00:21:16,760 --> 00:21:18,860
These games will ship throughout the world.

354
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:21,480
We export about 40% of our games.

355
00:21:21,880 --> 00:21:23,920
Today we're making Austrian games,

356
00:21:24,100 --> 00:21:27,900
so they have to be set up for Austrian coinage, actually Euros,

357
00:21:27,901 --> 00:21:30,200
and with German language and what have you.

358
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:34,080
While pinball games have changed a lot over the years,

359
00:21:34,460 --> 00:21:36,160
there's one key element that hasn't.

360
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:38,240
The pinball.

361
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:42,060
It's 1 1⁄16 inches in diameter,

362
00:21:42,580 --> 00:21:45,380
weighs 0.17 pounds.

363
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:47,400
It's going to move along the play field

364
00:21:47,401 --> 00:21:49,240
at 100 inches per minute.

365
00:21:49,980 --> 00:21:51,720
These were originally just ball bearings.

366
00:21:51,980 --> 00:21:54,000
I don't know if they're used for anything else now.

367
00:21:54,460 --> 00:21:57,240
I do know that our manufacturer has moved offshore

368
00:21:57,241 --> 00:22:00,440
and we had a little problem getting these for a while,

369
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:02,680
which would have been the end of the pinball business.

370
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:07,060
For decades, making a gleaming pinball roll around

371
00:22:07,061 --> 00:22:09,980
and rack up points has endlessly fascinated gamers.

372
00:22:11,760 --> 00:22:14,500
But other arcade machines turn your shiny coins

373
00:22:14,501 --> 00:22:15,820
into the game piece.

374
00:22:18,460 --> 00:22:20,160
When inserted into the machine,

375
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:22,640
they don't fall directly into the coin box,

376
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:24,760
but become part of the play,

377
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:26,960
adding a little edge to winning or losing.

378
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:31,240
When customers insert their coins into a certain variety of machine,

379
00:22:31,241 --> 00:22:35,240
they hope they will cause other coins to fall off shelves.

380
00:22:35,241 --> 00:22:41,240
But all those coins teetering on the edge tempt some into not playing fair.

381
00:22:41,241 --> 00:22:44,240
Now you get a lot of people who are always trying to cheat the arcade and the game.

382
00:22:44,241 --> 00:22:47,240
They think, give it a good swift shake, and you'll win some of those.

383
00:22:47,241 --> 00:22:51,240
But as you see, you shake it, the alarm goes off, those quarters fall down,

384
00:22:51,241 --> 00:22:53,240
but you don't get any tickets.

385
00:22:53,241 --> 00:22:58,240
In arcades, you win tickets instead of coins.

386
00:22:58,241 --> 00:23:00,240
No gambling allowed.

387
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:04,240
Of course, countless fortunes have been deposited

388
00:23:04,241 --> 00:23:07,240
into casino slot machines by those hoping to turn

389
00:23:07,241 --> 00:23:11,240
a small investment into big winnings.

390
00:23:11,241 --> 00:23:14,240
But for some reason, in an arcade,

391
00:23:14,241 --> 00:23:17,240
inserting way too many quarters to pluck a puny prize

392
00:23:17,241 --> 00:23:21,240
out of those claw machines is considered a victory.

393
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:27,460
Before legislation, operators were able to change the tension on the claws

394
00:23:27,461 --> 00:23:31,280
or program the machine to allow a win every certain number of plays.

395
00:23:32,380 --> 00:23:36,520
Now everybody must get an equal, if still slim, chance of winning.

396
00:23:37,380 --> 00:23:42,820
When OK Manufacturing, the gumball guys, decided to manufacture a crane game,

397
00:23:42,821 --> 00:23:47,040
they came up with a kinder, gentler variation called tractor time.

398
00:23:49,900 --> 00:23:53,080
If you don't win a prize, it will keep going.

399
00:23:53,240 --> 00:23:57,240
It senses there's no prize, and in our machines,

400
00:23:57,241 --> 00:24:01,240
because they're winner every time, you get a second chance.

401
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:09,240
A sensor and an eye will see the product pass through.

402
00:24:09,241 --> 00:24:13,240
Once the eye sees the product pass through here,

403
00:24:13,241 --> 00:24:17,240
it activates and tells the machine to stop.

404
00:24:23,240 --> 00:24:28,680
There's a blue-collar side to coin-operative machines.

405
00:24:28,681 --> 00:24:31,240
They provide all kinds of services,

406
00:24:31,241 --> 00:24:38,080
from lockers and luggage cart rentals to laundromats.

407
00:24:38,081 --> 00:24:40,800
Today, there are about 18,000 laundromats

408
00:24:40,801 --> 00:24:45,200
in the United States, a 20% decline since 2005.

409
00:24:45,201 --> 00:24:47,600
The Census Bureau attributes this steep drop

410
00:24:47,601 --> 00:24:51,000
to more washers and dryers being offered in apartment units.

411
00:24:51,001 --> 00:24:54,000
But this isn't to say that they aren't profitable.

412
00:24:54,001 --> 00:24:58,000
The average machine can bring in $20,000 to $30,000 a year.

413
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:04,000
Coin-operative machines are available to help clean out and wash our cars.

414
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:09,000
But then again, it's because of our cars that we had to start paying for air,

415
00:25:09,001 --> 00:25:13,000
and also must deal with many people's least favorite coin-operative machine.

416
00:25:13,001 --> 00:25:20,000
There are currently over 5 million parking meters in the United States.

417
00:25:20,001 --> 00:25:25,000
The first one was installed in Oklahoma City on July 16, 1935.

418
00:25:25,001 --> 00:25:29,000
For the first 50 years, they were all mechanical.

419
00:25:29,001 --> 00:25:34,000
But over the past few years, they are quickly becoming obsolete.

420
00:25:34,001 --> 00:25:39,000
What we have here is a parking meter that was made in the 1950s.

421
00:25:39,001 --> 00:25:41,940
parking meter that was made in the 1950s.

422
00:25:41,941 --> 00:25:43,840
This is a parko meter.

423
00:25:43,841 --> 00:25:47,340
This has a big main spring in the meter.

424
00:25:47,341 --> 00:25:49,140
The technician would come by weekly

425
00:25:49,141 --> 00:25:52,080
and put the crank inside the back of the meter

426
00:25:52,081 --> 00:25:54,480
and would wind the timer and they would store energy

427
00:25:54,481 --> 00:25:55,960
for the remainder of the week.

428
00:25:57,180 --> 00:25:59,960
Other mechanical meters let the customer do the winding

429
00:25:59,961 --> 00:26:01,380
after inserting each coin.

430
00:26:02,920 --> 00:26:06,660
Then in the mid 80s, there was a new meter on the block.

431
00:26:06,661 --> 00:26:13,340
In 1985, the city of Beverly Hills began to replace their mechanical meters with this first-generation electronic meter citywide.

432
00:26:13,920 --> 00:26:19,060
The benefits are the single-coin slot for all of the coins, less moving parts,

433
00:26:19,460 --> 00:26:22,780
and an electronic log of the valid and invalid coins that are inserted in the meter.

434
00:26:23,020 --> 00:26:26,920
Because it's electronic, it does take a 9-volt battery, which we replace about once a year.

435
00:26:28,680 --> 00:26:33,560
Beverly Hills, California, currently has 3,100 meters in operation,

436
00:26:33,561 --> 00:26:36,980
and they bring in about $2.5 million a year.

437
00:26:39,260 --> 00:26:40,900
Will that be cash or credit?

438
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:43,900
The city of Beverly Hills was the first city in the United States

439
00:26:43,901 --> 00:26:45,840
to deploy a credit card meter on street.

440
00:26:46,200 --> 00:26:48,700
It still accepts coin, but you can insert your credit card.

441
00:26:51,140 --> 00:26:53,580
You can add more or less time.

442
00:26:54,020 --> 00:26:56,700
Press OK and the meter will process your credit card immediately.

443
00:26:57,180 --> 00:27:00,400
It also has a solar panel and it's capable of text messaging

444
00:27:00,401 --> 00:27:02,280
our enforcement and our maintenance personnel

445
00:27:02,281 --> 00:27:04,340
if the meter is vandalized or jammed.

446
00:27:06,220 --> 00:27:08,120
While parking meters are surviving

447
00:27:08,121 --> 00:27:09,960
and thriving in the 21st century,

448
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:13,820
pay phones are barely hanging on.

449
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:17,820
But there was a time when the pay phone was indispensable.

450
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:20,800
In 1964, when the U.S. Mint was ready

451
00:27:20,801 --> 00:27:23,440
to change the metallic composition of U.S. coins,

452
00:27:23,980 --> 00:27:25,540
they consulted with the Bell System

453
00:27:25,541 --> 00:27:27,440
and other interested parties

454
00:27:27,441 --> 00:27:29,780
to ensure that the new coins would still work

455
00:27:29,781 --> 00:27:31,800
in the public pay phone system of the U.S.

456
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:40,280
The first payphone, called a coin phone back then, went into service in Chicago in 1898.

457
00:27:40,281 --> 00:27:44,280
By 1902, there were over 80,000 in the United States.

458
00:27:44,281 --> 00:27:47,280
In 1910, there was even a portable model.

459
00:27:47,281 --> 00:27:53,280
This is an example of a three-slot payphone that is not mounted on a wall or found in a phone booth.

460
00:27:53,281 --> 00:27:56,280
It has a handle. It's made out of aluminum. It's fairly lightweight.

461
00:27:56,281 --> 00:28:01,280
It could be carried all over the Wanamaker department store for whom it was made.

462
00:28:01,281 --> 00:28:07,260
It was made so that the store employees could carry it into the restaurant or to other parts of the store for customer use.

463
00:28:07,620 --> 00:28:10,660
It had a long cord that could be plugged in wherever there was a phone jack.

464
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:18,280
In the 1920s and 30s, special devices turned the regular phones of small businesses into pay phones.

465
00:28:18,680 --> 00:28:25,760
If I take the receiver off the hook in preparation for using the phone and drop a nickel in the slot and press the lever,

466
00:28:25,761 --> 00:28:29,520
it pulls the little arm or fork out of the dial,

467
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:32,720
leaving the dial free to turn and for me to dial my call.

468
00:28:32,840 --> 00:28:35,620
When I'm finished with the call, I place the receiver on the hook

469
00:28:35,621 --> 00:28:39,980
and the arm returns to the lock position in the dial.

470
00:28:40,400 --> 00:28:42,920
And it's ready for the next person to come along and make a call.

471
00:28:43,160 --> 00:28:45,920
Here's a good example of a three-slot pay phone from the 50s.

472
00:28:46,100 --> 00:28:48,640
A local call would cost a dime or a nickel.

473
00:28:49,180 --> 00:28:52,120
If you wanted to make a long-distance call, you had to get the operator involved.

474
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:53,540
That'll be 35 cents.

475
00:28:53,541 --> 00:28:59,340
The most surprising thing about commercial pay phones, however, is how simple the coin mechanisms were.

476
00:29:00,280 --> 00:29:05,200
All the way up to 1965, before electronics were first incorporated.

477
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:14,860
And by getting the operator involved, she would be listening to a series of sounds as you put the coins through.

478
00:29:17,300 --> 00:29:22,580
In order for her to put the call through, she would listen, and they made distinctive sounds.

479
00:29:22,581 --> 00:29:26,060
A quarter would go through the slot and go right to a bong sound.

480
00:29:27,440 --> 00:29:29,200
And she'd know that you put a quarter in.

481
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:30,880
Next would be the dime.

482
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:33,500
She would listen for two ding sounds.

483
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:39,160
So that actually came around and hit the top of the bell and the bottom of the bell.

484
00:29:39,960 --> 00:29:40,700
Remarkably simple.

485
00:29:41,440 --> 00:29:43,800
Then you put the nickel in and it'll hit the bell one time.

486
00:29:45,440 --> 00:29:49,060
The phone had no idea of knowing how much money was collected.

487
00:29:49,061 --> 00:29:52,360
It was up to the operator and she kept count at the central office.

488
00:29:52,580 --> 00:29:55,060
and then she would go ahead and put your call through.

489
00:29:55,480 --> 00:29:59,140
In the 1990s, the number of payphones in the United States

490
00:29:59,141 --> 00:30:01,140
reached over 2.6 million.

491
00:30:02,340 --> 00:30:04,800
But because of the explosion of cell phone usage,

492
00:30:04,801 --> 00:30:06,980
the payphone is fading fast,

493
00:30:07,400 --> 00:30:09,740
with only roughly 100,000 of them remaining.

494
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:18,120
Many coin-operated machines are becoming less reliant on coins

495
00:30:18,121 --> 00:30:20,860
and more on alternative payment methods.

496
00:30:20,861 --> 00:30:23,020
Bill acceptors were only the beginning.

497
00:30:23,021 --> 00:30:26,440
Now many apartment tenants can simply swipe a smart card

498
00:30:26,441 --> 00:30:28,900
to do their laundry, and vending machines selling

499
00:30:28,901 --> 00:30:31,900
high-priced items may only take credit cards.

500
00:30:31,901 --> 00:30:34,900
Here at Macy's, we found that our customers really enjoy

501
00:30:34,901 --> 00:30:36,940
getting their gadgets from the vending machine.

502
00:30:36,941 --> 00:30:38,980
It's really quick and easy, and you can buy it

503
00:30:38,981 --> 00:30:40,980
within a matter of seconds.

504
00:30:40,981 --> 00:30:43,820
It's estimated that the average U.S. household has about

505
00:30:43,821 --> 00:30:47,860
$113 of spare change just sitting around.

506
00:30:47,861 --> 00:30:51,860
Nationwide, that's about $15 billion worth.

507
00:30:51,861 --> 00:30:54,860
The CoinStar company found a way to cash in on the public's

508
00:30:54,861 --> 00:30:57,860
coin reserves by placing its high-speed counting machines

509
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:00,860
in supermarkets and other retail outlets.

510
00:31:00,861 --> 00:31:03,860
In the 17 years CoinStar has been in business,

511
00:31:03,861 --> 00:31:07,860
it has processed over 350 billion coins,

512
00:31:07,861 --> 00:31:11,860
with an average transaction of $68 at each CoinStar machine.

513
00:31:11,861 --> 00:31:13,860
Right from the beginning of CoinStar,

514
00:31:13,861 --> 00:31:15,860
we networked all of our machines,

515
00:31:15,861 --> 00:31:20,100
So we know all the information from every single machine right across the world.

516
00:31:20,101 --> 00:31:25,500
And our largest ever transaction was $13,000 in pennies from a gentleman in Alabama.

517
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:31,580
CoinStar's main manufacturing facility is located in Kent, Washington.

518
00:31:31,581 --> 00:31:34,540
Workers assemble about 1,000 machines a year here.

519
00:31:35,820 --> 00:31:37,380
The CoinStar machine is very simple to use.

520
00:31:37,381 --> 00:31:42,660
You bring your coin jar to the machine, follow the prompts, choose your language.

521
00:31:42,661 --> 00:31:48,660
And the coin machine asks you to put your coins directly into the machine.

522
00:31:48,661 --> 00:31:53,160
This is the first stage of actually cleaning the coins.

523
00:31:53,161 --> 00:31:55,780
As the consumers bring these in, you'd be shocked at what we see in here.

524
00:31:55,781 --> 00:32:00,580
We see paper clips, washers, we see all kinds of gum, everything else.

525
00:32:00,581 --> 00:32:04,420
As they put it in here, notice that there are holes in here that are smaller than any

526
00:32:04,421 --> 00:32:06,420
of the coins.

527
00:32:06,421 --> 00:32:10,660
Then it goes through this piece where you have a turning mechanism here that actually

528
00:32:10,661 --> 00:32:13,900
is beating the coins apart, and as they beat the coins apart,

529
00:32:13,901 --> 00:32:15,900
also any debris that'll fall out of there.

530
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:20,260
I'm now going to open up the machine,

531
00:32:20,261 --> 00:32:21,980
and once all the debris has been removed

532
00:32:21,981 --> 00:32:24,040
through the cleaning process, all of the coins

533
00:32:24,041 --> 00:32:27,340
drop down into the coin counting device at the bottom here.

534
00:32:27,341 --> 00:32:31,080
This is our coin counting device outside of a machine.

535
00:32:31,081 --> 00:32:33,120
Over here behind this fancy black box

536
00:32:33,121 --> 00:32:36,120
is our intellectual property that we use

537
00:32:36,121 --> 00:32:38,820
to actually run this device.

538
00:32:38,821 --> 00:32:41,860
The consumer comes up, they drop their coins in.

539
00:32:41,861 --> 00:32:46,320
As it goes through here, you'll notice that this machine starts running through here.

540
00:32:46,321 --> 00:32:52,160
Each one of these arms are picking up coins and running it down this coin rail.

541
00:32:52,161 --> 00:32:55,200
As it runs through the coin rail, notice how the coins are separating.

542
00:32:55,201 --> 00:32:58,640
This allows time for each coin to be sensed.

543
00:32:58,641 --> 00:33:03,720
This sensor knows that it's a penny, a nickel, a dime, a quarter, half dollar, a dollar.

544
00:33:03,721 --> 00:33:08,680
And actually, you'll recognize that and keep track of that in our system.

545
00:33:08,681 --> 00:33:13,840
Just past the coin sensor is a metal plate, moving too fast to be seen by the naked eye.

546
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:21,400
When the sensor detects a good coin, the plate jumps forward and sends it down a chute, leading to the holding bins below.

547
00:33:22,780 --> 00:33:31,620
The plate doesn't jump when foreign coins and non-valid objects like slugs are sensed, and they fall down another chute, so customers can recover their rejects.

548
00:33:31,621 --> 00:33:36,120
At the end of every transaction, a voucher is produced, a fee is charged for using the

549
00:33:36,121 --> 00:33:39,740
service, and you then take the voucher to the checkout to exchange for cash.

550
00:33:39,741 --> 00:33:43,860
Or you can choose a gift card now and get free coin counting.

551
00:33:43,861 --> 00:33:48,200
The counted coins are held inside the machine in two secure steel bins.

552
00:33:48,201 --> 00:33:50,580
Each bin holds over 500 pounds of coin.

553
00:33:50,581 --> 00:33:53,140
Our network tells us when to collect these bins.

554
00:33:53,141 --> 00:33:56,660
We have special moving devices that allow our companies to come in and collect these

555
00:33:56,661 --> 00:34:00,660
coins and take them directly to the processing centers for counting.

556
00:34:00,661 --> 00:34:06,900
In the Los Angeles area, trucks bring Coinstar's bins to a coin processing center, operated

557
00:34:06,901 --> 00:34:09,440
by Brinks.

558
00:34:09,441 --> 00:34:14,020
The driver rolls the bins off the truck into a secure loading dock, in which the inner

559
00:34:14,021 --> 00:34:18,740
and outer doors are never opened at the same time.

560
00:34:18,741 --> 00:34:23,740
Once all the full bins are exchanged for empty ones, the inner door opens and the full bins

561
00:34:23,741 --> 00:34:26,980
are rolled into the facility.

562
00:34:26,981 --> 00:34:31,860
Six employees place each bin on an elevator and then unlock it so the coins can pour into

563
00:34:31,861 --> 00:34:35,200
a sorting device.

564
00:34:35,201 --> 00:34:40,800
Vibrating disks full of holes eliminate anything bigger or smaller than U.S. coins.

565
00:34:40,801 --> 00:34:45,440
Then they travel up a conveyor and into a counting and sorting mechanism.

566
00:34:45,441 --> 00:34:51,420
These machines here have the capability of processing approximately 5,000 to 8,000 coins

567
00:34:51,421 --> 00:34:52,420
per minute.

568
00:34:52,421 --> 00:34:54,960
Not only is it verifying and counting the coin,

569
00:34:54,961 --> 00:34:59,800
but also it's separating the coin by denomination.

570
00:34:59,801 --> 00:35:03,860
Each denomination pours down a tube and into a bin.

571
00:35:03,861 --> 00:35:07,740
When a bin is full, it's moved over to a scale.

572
00:35:07,741 --> 00:35:11,000
They get weight to verify that the appropriate amount of money

573
00:35:11,001 --> 00:35:15,740
that was processed is actually in the bin.

574
00:35:15,741 --> 00:35:18,920
When it's time to prepare the coins for recirculation,

575
00:35:18,921 --> 00:35:21,820
workers dump them into another series of machines,

576
00:35:21,821 --> 00:35:24,320
each designed to handle a specific denomination.

577
00:35:26,600 --> 00:35:28,360
This machine is counting quarters,

578
00:35:29,580 --> 00:35:32,060
organizing them into a steady stream

579
00:35:32,061 --> 00:35:35,120
and inserting 40 quarters at a time into a plastic sleeve.

580
00:35:38,320 --> 00:35:40,320
Now human hands take over

581
00:35:40,321 --> 00:35:44,020
and place the plastic-wrapped tubes of coins into boxes

582
00:35:44,021 --> 00:35:47,140
and box after box onto pallets.

583
00:35:47,141 --> 00:35:48,860
This is the largest coin center in the world.

584
00:35:48,861 --> 00:35:50,880
We process over three billion coins

585
00:35:50,881 --> 00:35:52,640
for coins to our launch.

586
00:35:52,641 --> 00:35:54,880
These boxes here are going back into circulation

587
00:35:54,881 --> 00:35:58,080
for our customers such as banks, supermarkets,

588
00:35:58,081 --> 00:36:01,220
and as well here we have canvas bags filled with coins

589
00:36:01,221 --> 00:36:05,420
that will be going to the Federal Reserve for recirculation.

590
00:36:05,421 --> 00:36:09,400
A circulating coin can last approximately 30 years.

591
00:36:09,401 --> 00:36:12,840
Once the Federal Reserve takes those coins out of circulation,

592
00:36:12,841 --> 00:36:16,140
they're replaced with new ones from the U.S. Mint.

593
00:36:16,141 --> 00:36:18,000
Once those coins are then minted,

594
00:36:18,001 --> 00:36:20,340
a new circulation cycle begins.

595
00:36:20,341 --> 00:36:22,600
So how long is the life expectancy of a penny?

596
00:36:23,400 --> 00:36:24,520
Just 12 years.

597
00:36:31,620 --> 00:36:36,560
Modern arcade games may offer wild interactivity and imaginative graphics.

598
00:36:37,520 --> 00:36:41,320
But some of the mechanical coin-operated amusements of the past

599
00:36:41,321 --> 00:36:48,400
deliver their own special brand of fascinating, eccentric, and often bizarre entertainment.

600
00:36:50,340 --> 00:36:54,840
At Musée Mécanique, located on Fisherman's Wharf in San Francisco,

601
00:36:55,640 --> 00:36:59,060
Dan Zielinski uses roller skates to race around a huge room.

602
00:37:00,740 --> 00:37:05,160
It houses the vast collection his late father began for the public to enjoy.

603
00:37:07,680 --> 00:37:10,440
Most of the machines will set you back just a quarter or two.

604
00:37:10,960 --> 00:37:14,900
Of all the machines that my father and I have collected here at the Musée Mécanique,

605
00:37:15,020 --> 00:37:16,720
this is the largest one in the collection.

606
00:37:16,721 --> 00:37:19,220
It was made in the 1930s,

607
00:37:19,221 --> 00:37:23,120
and it was all hand-carved by a single individual.

608
00:37:23,121 --> 00:37:25,860
This is a great deal for the public in general.

609
00:37:25,861 --> 00:37:28,960
For 50 cents, you can get 10, 20, or 30 people

610
00:37:28,961 --> 00:37:31,060
to crowd around this and watch the entire thing

611
00:37:31,061 --> 00:37:32,960
come to life at the drop of a coin.

612
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:40,680
Usually, the appeal of the machines is obvious.

613
00:37:40,681 --> 00:37:43,780
Who doesn't enjoy an all-monkey band?

614
00:37:43,781 --> 00:37:45,320
There are some machines that must have been

615
00:37:45,321 --> 00:37:50,280
been a bit of a letdown when customers first had the chance to put their hard-earned coins in them.

616
00:37:52,840 --> 00:37:55,160
But other curiosities are well worth the investment.

617
00:37:57,400 --> 00:38:00,230
This circus is made entirely out of toothpicks.

618
00:38:00,231 --> 00:38:03,000
Obviously, this sort of intricate work is done

619
00:38:03,001 --> 00:38:06,905
by someone with a lot of spare time on their hands.

620
00:38:06,906 --> 00:38:10,120
This is a toothpick ferris wheel. It's not

621
00:38:10,121 --> 00:38:14,920
terribly elaborate, but what makes it interesting is it was made by a San Quentin prisoner.

622
00:38:15,320 --> 00:38:21,240
It's part of a larger display that was constructed of over 150,000 toothpicks.

623
00:38:22,480 --> 00:38:26,460
The vintage oddities at the Musée Mécanique are certainly unique.

624
00:38:27,420 --> 00:38:34,460
But a search for truly weird coin-operated fare inevitably brings you to Farmington Hills, Michigan.

625
00:38:35,400 --> 00:38:39,520
Welcome to Marvin's, known from coast to coast like butter and toast.

626
00:38:40,080 --> 00:38:44,400
Nowhere in the contiguous United States or other environs is a place like Marvin's.

627
00:38:44,401 --> 00:38:46,740
Welcome. Have fun.

628
00:38:46,741 --> 00:38:51,240
Just about every inch of ceiling, wall, and floor space

629
00:38:51,241 --> 00:38:53,340
is jam-packed.

630
00:38:53,341 --> 00:38:56,120
The museum is over 5,000 square feet

631
00:38:56,121 --> 00:38:58,540
and has over 1,000 electrical outlets

632
00:38:58,541 --> 00:39:01,280
to power all the historic and modern machines.

633
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:06,260
His interest in these mechanical wonders

634
00:39:06,261 --> 00:39:08,220
began about 50 years ago.

635
00:39:08,221 --> 00:39:10,460
When I was in college, I was fascinated

636
00:39:10,461 --> 00:39:13,560
by these coin-operated machines, and I started collecting.

637
00:39:13,561 --> 00:39:17,260
Well, after I graduated in the early 60s, I couldn't quit collecting.

638
00:39:17,840 --> 00:39:20,280
He started collecting and collecting this stuff.

639
00:39:20,380 --> 00:39:22,040
He overtook my mother's house.

640
00:39:22,600 --> 00:39:24,680
1980, she took me to a food court and thought,

641
00:39:24,820 --> 00:39:28,140
what a great place for your dad to get this stuff out of my house

642
00:39:28,141 --> 00:39:30,060
and put it for other people to enjoy.

643
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:32,900
Marvin is the ultimate collector,

644
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:35,500
always on the lookout for, well,

645
00:39:36,180 --> 00:39:39,640
unusual coin-operated contraptions to add to his museum.

646
00:39:39,641 --> 00:39:46,760
These wooden automata, the craftsmen are still making these machines there, so there's

647
00:39:46,761 --> 00:39:53,900
a place to buy old ones and new ones, which I like equally well.

648
00:39:53,901 --> 00:39:59,760
Many of the old Penny Arcade machines have a decidedly dark bet.

649
00:39:59,761 --> 00:40:05,740
Intricate motors and gears cause small robotic figures to act out all sorts of ghoulish scenes,

650
00:40:05,741 --> 00:40:08,840
including the horrors of the Spanish Inquisition.

651
00:40:08,841 --> 00:40:11,180
In keeping with this grisly tradition,

652
00:40:11,181 --> 00:40:14,500
Marvin decided his customers might enjoy firing up

653
00:40:14,501 --> 00:40:16,000
an electric chair.

654
00:40:16,001 --> 00:40:19,520
Now this machine I found is a Halloween prop,

655
00:40:19,521 --> 00:40:24,360
and I added a case and a coin-accepted mechanism and watch.

656
00:40:24,361 --> 00:40:38,361
It may be a little too intense for younger children.

657
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:44,000
Another machine turns the tables on Marvin's customers

658
00:40:44,001 --> 00:40:46,680
and delivers a shocking experience.

659
00:40:47,460 --> 00:40:49,900
This machine is Challenge Killer Watt,

660
00:40:50,080 --> 00:40:51,640
machine unique to my museum.

661
00:40:51,641 --> 00:40:55,820
It was built by a friend of mine, a robotics engineer, Ohio.

662
00:40:56,380 --> 00:41:02,220
And the idea of the machine is to keep your hands as long as you can on this.

663
00:41:03,220 --> 00:41:08,060
A cute little hero to take on kilowatt, huh?

664
00:41:08,420 --> 00:41:10,860
Actually, a little static electricity is produced.

665
00:41:11,620 --> 00:41:14,960
Very light and very non-dangerous.

666
00:41:15,140 --> 00:41:16,360
But it does give you a little tingle.

667
00:41:16,361 --> 00:41:19,040
Don't worry. Try a little of this.

668
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:20,700
Whoa!

669
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:25,720
In the old days, there were some machines that did give you a shock.

670
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:32,240
But of course, in today's litigious society, we can't have anything like that.

671
00:41:32,820 --> 00:41:37,740
From pinball to fortune tellers, it's easy to spend a small fortune in coins here.

672
00:41:38,200 --> 00:41:44,300
But with the fun and excitement these machines provide, it's money well spent.

